# Line endings that are load-bearing.
#
# A shell script whose shebang ends in CRLF fails with
#   env: bash\r: No such file or directory
# because /usr/bin/env looks for a program whose name contains a carriage return.
# Git for Windows converts line endings on checkout by default, so without this a
# clone on Windows produces a launcher that cannot run on macOS or Linux — and the
# person who built the zip never sees it.
*.command  text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
launcher.py text eol=lf

# And the other direction. These two are only ever read by Windows, and cmd.exe and
# Windows Script Host both want CRLF — a `.bat` with LF endings mis-parses multi-line
# blocks, and pinning it here means the zip is correct whichever platform built it.
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.vbs      text eol=crlf

# Binary, so Git never tries to be helpful with them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.bin binary
*.zip binary
